{#- Atlas — surface two: triage on a phone (decision 27).

  Not a squeezed depth chart. On a phone the question is never "how far has this got" but "why has
  it stopped", so the cards are ordered by what needs the owner and every card ends on its gate.

  The order decision 27 fixes:

    1. waiting on a decision — the workstream is designed but not approved, or its next milestone
       is blocked, or nothing is left on record for it to do
    2. moving   — a milestone is in flight
    3. blocked  — work is parked on something outside the workstream
    4. designing
    5. not started

  This layout classifies nothing and sorts nothing: `src/triage.mjs` decides both, and hands the
  cards over already in order. That is deliberate. `state.json` (decision 29) is built from the
  same function, so the machine-readable output and this page cannot disagree about what needs the
  owner — and a mapping that lives in a template is one no table test can pin and no other surface
  can reuse.

  Data this layout expects:
    triaged — orderByTriage(...)'s output: the workstreams already in card order, each one a
              { slug, manifest, triage, column }, where `triage` is its state and `column` is its
              own entry from computeLadder's columns — including `covered`, `completedCount` and
              `milestoneCount`, which are what this page draws the track from
    project, repo, title — see base.njk
-#}
{% extends "base.njk" %}
{% import "base.njk" as ui %}

{% block bodyClass %}page page-mobile{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<h1>What needs you</h1>
<p class="lede">Ordered by what is waiting on a decision, not by name. Every card ends on the gate that is holding it.</p>

<ul class="card-list">
{%- for stream in triaged %}
<li>
<article class="card" data-workstream="{{ stream.manifest.codename }}" data-triage="{{ stream.triage }}">
<header class="card-head">
<h2><a href="{{ stream.url }}">{{ stream.manifest.codename }}</a></h2>
{{ ui.chip("triage", stream.triage) }}
</header>
<p class="card-what">{{ stream.manifest.what }}</p>
<p class="card-position">{{ stream.manifest.position }}</p>
{#- How much of the track is filled. Not counted here: `computeLadder` decides what "complete"
    means for a workstream (decision 24 — the contiguous run from depth 1, so a done M2 behind an
    un-done M1 completes nothing), and the depth chart draws its bar from the same numbers. This
    page reads them. A second count here is exactly how these two surfaces came to state
    contradictory facts about one workstream. -#}
{% if stream.column.milestoneCount %}
<div class="track" role="img" aria-label="{{ stream.column.completedCount }} of {{ stream.column.milestoneCount }} milestones complete">
{% for filled in stream.column.covered %}<span class="track-seg{% if filled %} is-filled{% endif %}"></span>{% endfor %}
</div>
<p class="track-count"><span class="num">{{ stream.column.completedCount }}</span> of <span class="num">{{ stream.column.milestoneCount }}</span> milestones complete</p>
{% else %}
<p class="track-count empty">No milestones on record yet</p>
{% endif %}
<p class="card-next">Next: {{ stream.column.tipLabel }}</p>
<p class="card-gate"><span class="card-gate-label">Gate</span>{{ stream.manifest.gate }}</p>
</article>
</li>
{% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endblock %}
